# Response headers for the static host — Cloudflare Pages reads this file, and
# Netlify reads the same format. Vite copies public/ into dist/, so it ships
# with every build. test/hosting.test.ts holds it to what is written here.
#
# One rule about the format decides how this file is laid out: when several
# rules match a path, the host sends the headers of ALL of them, and the same
# header named twice is joined into one comma-separated value. So Cache-Control
# is never set on /* — "no-cache" there would be glued onto "immutable" below —
# and each path that needs one gets exactly one rule that sets it.

# On every path:
#
# Content-Security-Policy — the one part of the policy a <meta> cannot carry:
# who may put these pages in a frame. 'self' because the landing page frames
# /app/ as its demo; nobody else, or a look-alike page could dress itself in
# the app. The rest of the policy stays in the <meta> every page already has
# (src/lib/csp.ts) and is NOT repeated here: a header and a meta are both
# enforced, so a copy that drifted would silently tighten the app, and a build
# given VITE_FEED_ORIGIN has a policy this one file cannot know.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options — a file is what it says it is. The worker already
# refuses HTML answering for a script (sw.js, `fits`); this stops the browser
# guessing either way.
#
# Referrer-Policy — shops' returns pages are opened with rel="noreferrer", and
# the privacy page says the app sends nothing with them, "not even that you
# came from Quids In". This makes that true of every request, not only of the
# links that remembered the attribute.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security — HTTPS only, for a year. Deliberately no
# includeSubDomains and no preload: both reach beyond this site, and preload
# cannot be quickly undone. Those are the domain owner's decisions.
#
# Permissions-Policy — only what the app uses. camera=(self) for receipt
# scanning: on the web that is a file input with capture="environment", which
# this does not gate, but a camera opened in a page must stay with this origin.
# web-share and clipboard-write=(self): the win card and the letters go out
# through the share sheet or the clipboard (App.tsx, Letter.tsx,
# SupportDetails.tsx). Everything else is off, the demo frame included.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), web-share=(self), clipboard-write=(self), clipboard-read=(), geolocation=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), serial=(), hid=(), bluetooth=(), midi=(), display-capture=(), accelerometer=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), browsing-topics=()

# Content-hashed: a changed file is a new name, so a copy may be kept for ever.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Everything below changes at a FIXED url, so it must be asked about again
# every time (no-cache = may be stored, must be revalidated; an unchanged file
# costs a 304).
#
# The pages matter most. The worker's launch is network first and goes through
# the HTTP cache, and a host deploys atomically, so last build's hashed files
# are gone the moment the new one is live. An /app/ cached for an hour would,
# for that hour, open a shell naming files the server no longer has. The
# worker script is checked past the HTTP cache by browsers anyway, and its
# precache and the stale-tab check (main.tsx) both ask with cache: 'no-cache';
# saying no-cache here too means no browser or proxy is left to disagree.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/app/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/app/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/privacy/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/privacy/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/rights/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/rights/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# What an installed app is: its name, its icons and its share target. The
# share target has changed method before (GET to POST, see sw.js), and the
# browser refreshes an install from whatever this answers.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The verified policy changes, and the signature that must cover exactly the
# bytes the feed answered with. The worker fetches both network first (sw.js,
# FRESH); an hour-old copy from the HTTP cache would undo that.
/policy-feed.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/policy-feed.sig
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Not content-hashed, and the icon has just changed in the rebrand, so
# "immutable" would pin the old one on every device that had seen it. A day
# instead: the font and the favicon are on every page, so a day spares a
# request per view, and a changed one still reaches everybody by tomorrow.
# The worker is not held to the day: it precaches both with
# cache: 'no-cache' at every install.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The receipt reader's worker, cores and model, at fixed paths because
# tesseract finds them by directory. Revalidated rather than kept for a day: a
# new worker.min.js read beside a day-old core of another version would break
# scanning, and a 304 costs nothing next to ten megabytes.
/ocr/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
